    "result_text": "Done. The build passes and all tests are green (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`; `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class \u2014 the other two files are the base/helper).\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned the owner identity key to `identityKey = SHA-256 hex over (normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName))` and made duplicate detection key off it.\n\n**New code**\n- `util/Soundex.java` \u2014 standard American Soundex (letter + 3 digits), used for the surname component. No new dependency; implemented directly since `commons-codec` wasn't on the classpath.\n\n**Changed**\n- `util/IdentityKey.java` \u2014 now returns the 64-hex SHA-256 digest over `normalizedTelephone | lowerEmail | soundex(lastName)` instead of the old pipe-joined `telephone|email|householdId` string. The `of(...)` overload now takes `(telephone, email, lastName)`.\n- `owner/EnsureUniqueIdentity.java` \u2014 the 409 block now compares the request's identity key against each non-deleted owner's key, instead of matching on the computed `householdId` + telephone. This removes the separate household-duplicate rule. The disposable-email blocklist still runs first (unchanged pipeline order), deleted owners are still skipped, and `sharesHousehold` still bypasses.\n- `owner/AssignPossibleDuplicate.java` \u2014 the soft match now fires when postcode matches **and** the last-name Soundex matches **but** the identity key differs. Because telephone is part of the key, two owners with the same last name + postcode but different telephones now flow through as a soft match rather than a hard 409.\n- `escalation/DuplicateIdentityException.java` \u2014 carries the `identityKey` (message `\"Owner already registered: <key>\"`); the handler is unchanged since it reads `getMessage()`.\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 updated the `ensureUniqueIdentity` and `flagPossibleDuplicate` step comments to match.\n\n`householdId` (and `AssignHousehold`/`Households`) is retained \u2014 it's still used for household sizing and membership-level capping \u2014 it just no longer drives the 409.",
